      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Oh, you're right, I got a bit carried away with the goblin metaphor there! I was just trying to say that the turbo is working overtime, like a goblin trying to squeeze every last drop of juice out of an apple, but that was a bit too much for a serious exam question.\n\nTo answer your question directly: that high-pitched whine is completely normal and is actually the sound of the compressor wheel spinning at high speeds, forcing air through the intake pipes. It's the engine's way of saying, \"I'm working hard to get that extra boost!\"\n\nHowever, if the sound is suddenly very loud, changes pitch abruptly, or sounds like it's grinding or rattling, that *could* be a sign of a problem, like worn bearings or a failing seal. In those cases, the turbo might be struggling to spin smoothly, which isn't good for the engine's health. But for most of the time, that whine is just the turbo working hard to boost your engine's performance.\n\nSo, for your exam: the whine is normal operation, but listen for grinding or sudden changes in pitch, as those might indicate mechanical failure.",
